@import docs from '../../layouts/docs.clarus'
@import callout from '../../components/callout.clarus'

<docs [title]="'Islands'">
  <h1>Islands</h1>
  <p>A component with a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> is interactive. It is rendered to HTML on the server like everything else, then <em>attached</em> in the browser: its event handlers and updates are wired to the HTML that is already there. Nothing re-renders.</p>

  <h2>State</h2>
  <p><code>state()</code> makes a value reactive and <code>derived()</code> computes from it. You read and write them as plain variables; the compiler rewrites <code>count++</code> into a signal update.</p>
  <pre><code>{{ `<script>
  let count = state(0)
  const double = derived(() => count * 2)
</script>

<button (click)="count++">{{ count }} × 2 = {{ double }}</button>` }}</code></pre>

  <h2>What ships to the browser</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A small loader, only on pages that have islands.</li>
    <li>One chunk per interactive component, with just its <code>attach</code> function.</li>
    <li>Nothing for components without a script, and nothing for the page itself.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This page has no islands, so it loads no JavaScript at all. The <a href="/">home page</a> has one.</p>

  <callout><p>In the HTML, an island is a <code>&lt;cl-island&gt;</code> element that names its chunk and props, and its reactive text sits between <code>&lt;!--[0--&gt;</code> markers. View the source of the home page to see it.</p></callout>
</docs>
